  "aiOverview": {
    "content": "Appendicitis is a medical emergency caused by an inflamed appendix, usually triggered by a blockage, resulting in sudden, severe abdominal pain that often moves from the belly button to the lower-right abdomen. Symptoms include vomiting, loss of appetite, fever, and abdominal tenderness. Seek immediate care for severe pain.Symptoms of AppendicitisPain: Often begins around the belly button and shifts to the lower right abdomen.Pain Intensity: Worsens with movement, coughing, or walking.Digestive Issues: Nausea, vomiting, loss of appetite, constipation, or diarrhea.Other: Low-grade fever, abdominal bloating, and inability to pass gas.Note: Pain may differ in children or during pregnancy.Causes of AppendicitisAppendicitis occurs when the appendix becomes blocked by:Hardened stool (fecaliths): The most common cause, where appendix stones block the opening.Lymphoid hyperplasia: Swollen lymphoid tissue, often following a viral infection.Colitis or tumors: Other sources of obstruction or infection.Result: The obstruction causes bacteria to multiply, leading to swelling, pus, and potential rupture.DiagnosisPhysical Exam: Checking for pain in the lower right abdomen.Blood and Urine Tests: To check for infection or pregnancy.Imaging Tests: Abdominal ultrasound or CT scan are commonly used to confirm inflammation.When to Seek Medical AttentionImmediately: If you or your child experience sudden, sharp abdominal pain that starts around the belly button and moves to the right side.Rupture Warning: If pain temporarily improves before getting significantly worse, and symptoms include a high fever or rigid abdomen, get emergency care.Risk FactorsAge: Most common in people aged 10–30.Sex: Slightly more common in males.Family History: Family history of the condition can increase risk.TreatmentAppendectomy: Surgery to remove the appendix is the standard treatment.Antibiotics: Sometimes used, but surgery is preferred to prevent rupture.If left untreated, an infected appendix can rupture in 48–72 hours, spreading infection in the abdomen (peritonitis), which can be fatal.",
